Child of uiai-engine-h6s (P1 Spec 104 adoption in UIAI Engine).

Current state (internal/observability/errors.go:60):

  var defaultStore = &ErrorStore{}

  func Record(event ErrorEvent) ErrorEvent { return defaultStore.Record(event) }
  func Recent(limit int, source, class string) []ErrorEvent {
      return defaultStore.Recent(limit, source, class)
  }
  func Count() int  { return defaultStore.Count() }
  func Clear()      { defaultStore.Clear() }

This is a process-global mutable ErrorStore. Different consumers (different FOCUSA projects, different host sessions) calling observability.Record() share the same 500-event bounded ledger. Two projects seeing the same error class will see each other\u0027s entries on /api/errors.

Options per Spec 104 \u00a75.5 rules:

(A) Scope-key the store. Pass ScopeRef to Record/Recent; route to per-scope sub-stores; cap per-scope instead of globally.
(B) Classify the store as runtime-infra singleton under \u00a75.5.5 exception (process health, telemetry) and explicitly document the bleed risk in code comments + docs/observability.md.
(C) Remove the singleton entirely by making ErrorStore a dependency injected from main.go and passed through Engine.

Recommended: (A) with a documented fallback to (B) if the per-scope indexing breaks UIAI\u0027s cross-project error correlation use case.

Acceptance:
- One of (A)/(B)/(C) is implemented and proven by a unit test.
- If (A): /api/errors requires scope query parameter; without scope returns 400 with explicit next_action.
- If (B): docs/observability.md explains the bleed risk and what consumers can do about it.
- If (C): all callers (internal/errors_test.go, routes/errors.go, vision/session.go writeSessionError) updated.

Evidence citations required on close: spec 104 PR hash + test artifact + a static grep that proves defaultStore is no longer referenced as a package-level singleton (if (A) or (C)) or that the exception is documented in code (if (B)).

Dependency: uiai-engine-h6s must land first.
